after a few hours with breakpoints  immersive mode I have to say there  definitely some big improvements but  also noticeable limitations let's talk  about what works and doesn't work and  what settings you can change to get the  game closer to the Ghost Recon you  probably want immersive is really more  of a series of settings than a game  overhaul if you're looking for huge  structural changes to break point there  is some good progress here like getting  rid of tiered loot and eliminating other  random players from the social spaces  but at the same time immersive further  highlights problems that have been there  from the start  particularly with bad enemy behavior  even when you're doing your best to play  realistically enemies often just won't  play along and there isn't a sweet spot  at any difficulty setting at the default  end of immersive mode you'll still be  mowing through bases like a one-man army  and at the hardest settings you'll die  horribly particularly in sections where  the game inexplicably surrounds you with  enemies right after a cutscene  you'll also still see enemy groups not  quite know how to handle you when you're  inside of buildings or simply not  respond when their buddies get shot  right next to them  maybe immersive mode just isn't the  right word for this the game's  interrogation mechanic for example  really breaks your immersion by letting  you shoot a bad guy in multiple times  before picking them back up and talking  to them like nothing happened this is  more of a robust hard mode for break  point all that being said if you use the  new mechanics in the open world  especially where you can be stealthy it  can really be enjoyable  everything you need to get the most out  of immersive mode is in the custom  settings the first thing to do is switch  to using a single primary weapon it's  honestly one of my favorite toggles  because not only does it look more  realistic but it forces you to choose a  role and that counts if you're playing  alone or with friends it makes you for  example decide of your a stealthy sniper  or a hard-charging assaulter if you  don't have a long-range gun suddenly  enemy snipers become way more dangerous  or if you just took a sniper rifle all  you're gonna have for close combat is a  pistol one quick note when you turn on  immersive mode to make sure you have the  gun you want equipped because it appears  to get rid of your other weapons that  aren't blueprints while the mode is  active the next thing you're gonna want  to do is go to the interface menu and  turn off anything that would pull you  out of the world like damage indicators  and crosshairs break points out is  strongest when you're navigating just  using your eyes and ears some indicators  are essential like objective markers but  they turn on and off on the fly  on that note make iron sights the  default none of that over-the-shoulder  aiming shenanigans next crank up the  difficulty despite its name immersive  Mo's default settings still leave you  way too powerful  multiple bad guys can get the drop on  you fire first and you'll still have  time to react heal and win turn enemy  difficulty to advanced and tactical  difficulty to elite the two settings  sounds similar but enemy difficulty  impacts enemy behavior and tactical  difficulty changes the way your  character experiences the world such as  reducing the number of bandages you can  carry increasing your chance of injury  draining your stamina more quickly  turning off health regeneration and  causing you to throw away any left over  ammo that you still have in your  magazine when you reload turning up the  difficulty definitely takes breakpoint  much closer to the survival game  originally pitched as mentioned above  don't use extreme setting for enemies  because the game has missions that  purposely surround you and it just  doesn't work now that you're playing a  much harder game there's a few things to  understand they've waxed suddenly have a  lot more meaning for example they are  now one of the only places that you can  swap weapons and only two blueprints  that you own  which makes chasing blueprints that much  more important if you run out of ammo  and aren't near one you'll have to  scavenge weapons off dead enemy soldiers  which is new and actually pretty cool  there's a very handy comparison menu  that pops up when you're thinking about  making the switch you may also want to  prepare for battles in a way that you  haven't had to before using crafting  menus make sure to farm resources and  keep your canteen topped off with fresh  water because your stamina will thank  you if there's one thing immersive mode  does do really well it's that it gets  you to slow down and exist in the world  not constantly having to chase looting  upgrades makes everything just a lot  more enjoyable you'll get the most out  of the new harder settings if you still  limit yourself a bit and do things like  wander the open world hit side missions  do investigations but stay away from  anything that channels you into places  where the enemy might not agree to be  immersive with you this whole operation  goes to [ __ ] if we're seen together  let us know in the comments below if  you've tried the new setting and how  they make or don't make breakpoint into  the game that you want for more coverage  of shooters including doom and Call of  Duty war zone head over to GameSpot  thanks for watching  all good here I think
 

